.venv/
node_modules/
frontend/build/
frontend/.svelte-kit/
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/

# Everything under data/ is content, and content does not go in the repo. The
# repo holds the app; this machine holds what you have done with it.
#
# That means all of it: your trees, because you edit them in the UI and do not
# want a commit per season switch; the event log and checklist, because they
# are the record of your practice rather than part of the program; your notes
# and photographs, because they are private; and the index, because it is a
# cache that rebuilds from the log.
#
# The consequence is that the repo is NOT a backup. Every file below exists in
# one copy on this disk unless something else copies it — see SYNC.md.
#
# Written `data/*` and not `data/` on purpose. It used to matter because a
# negation followed it, and git does not descend into an excluded *directory*
# — so `!data/seed/` would never have been reached. The negation is gone with
# the tech tree; the form stays, because the day something under data/ has to
# be tracked again is the day that distinction bites, silently.
data/*

# The same, for a data directory that has moved somewhere else and been left
# behind until its owner is sure. `data/*` does not cover a sibling name, and
# what gets left behind is by definition the whole of someone's history.
data.*/

# There is no exception any more. `data/seed/` held the six researched
# curricula a fresh clone copied into data/domains/, and it went with the tech
# tree — so `data/` is now what the comment above says without qualification:
# entirely the user's, entirely outside the repo. Nothing under it is tracked,
# and `scripts/fixture.py` is where sample data comes from instead.

# The Trophic porting bundle: a copy of another repo's source, its schema, its
# UI notes and 2058 golden fixtures. Read once, ported from, and not part of
# this program — so it stays a local working copy rather than 17k lines of
# someone else's TypeScript in this history.
#
# The cost is real and worth knowing: `golden/verify_golden.py` lives inside
# it, so the port's oracle — and the parser adapter already wired into it —
# exist only on this disk. TROPHIC.md records where to fetch the bundle again
# and what to re-wire if it is ever re-copied.
#
# Leading slash, and it matters: an unanchored `trophic/` matches a directory
# of that name at *any* depth, which silently excluded the entire ported UI in
# frontend/src/lib/trophic/ and frontend/src/routes/trophic/.
/trophic/

# The design bundle the Log/Capture/Settings redesign was built from: prototype
# HTML frames, a README naming every token, and a copy of the bound Modernist
# design system. Ignored for the same reason as the bundle above — it is a
# read-once reference produced elsewhere, not part of this program, and the
# built screens are now the authority on what shipped. What it decided is kept
# where it is load-bearing: the light palette in
# `frontend/src/lib/trophic/colors.ts`, the two model switches in
# `settings.svelte.ts`, and the shape of the Log in CLAUDE.md.
/design_handoff_trophic_ui/

# The skills installed by `npx skills add` are tracked now, as ordinary
# directories under .claude/skills/ next to this repo's own design-study. They
# were ignored for one concrete reason and it no longer holds: the installer
# writes the files to .agents/skills/<name>/ and leaves .claude/skills/<name>
# as an *absolute* symlink into /c/Trophic-PGS/..., which would arrive dead on
# the other half of the dual boot. Copying the files where Claude Code looks
# for them and deleting .agents/ removes the symlink, so the skills now cross
# by git like any other file. `skills-lock.json` is tracked with them and
# records source and hash for each.
#
# What stays ignored is .agents/ itself, because a later `npx skills add` on
# this machine will recreate it — a second copy of skills that are already in
# the tree, plus symlinks that would overwrite the tracked directories. If that
# happens, take the update out of .agents/skills/<name>/ by hand and delete the
# rest. Updating in place is the price of having them travel.
/.agents/
